Day 77 - Trapping Rain Water

Problem Statement:
Given n non-negative integers representing an elevation map where the width of each bar is 1, calculate how much water can be trapped after raining.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    int trap(vector<int>& height) {
        int ans = 0;
        int n = height.size();

        vector<int> lmax(n);
        vector<int> rmax(n);

        lmax[0] = height[0];
        rmax[n - 1] = height[n - 1];

        for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            lmax[i] = max(lmax[i - 1], height[i]);
        }

        for(int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            rmax[i] = max(rmax[i + 1], height[i]);
        }

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            ans += min(lmax[i], rmax[i]) - height[i];
        }

        return ans;
    }
};



Time Complexity:
O(n), because we traverse the array three times.

Space Complexity:
O(n), for the lmax and rmax arrays.

Approach:
We use two arrays to store the maximum height on the left and right of every position.

lmax[i] → maximum height from the left up to index i.
rmax[i] → maximum height from the right up to index i.

For every position, the amount of water it can store is:

min(lmax[i], rmax[i]) - height[i]

The smaller of the left and right maximum heights determines the water level.

Finally, add the water stored at every position to ans.
